## Smallest robust change

Keep the three authored files as node:test. Add one small repository-relative runner (proposed scripts/counting-browser-tests.mjs), append the browser type project to root typecheck, and invoke that runner once at the start of root test. Keep scripts/gate.sh and every existing Vitest project/config/include/group/worker setting unchanged, including actual C0's counting definition project. Keep active-source's existing check. No browser, Worker, provider or speech process belongs to this runner.

Actual C0 af336 already adds examples/counting/tsconfig.json to typecheck; it includes only root *.ts. Append `&& tsc -p examples/counting/browser/tsconfig.json` after that check. Current root test is `vitest run && node --test scripts/active-source.test.mjs`. Proposed ordering is `node scripts/counting-browser-tests.mjs && vitest run && node --test scripts/active-source.test.mjs`: cheap frontend source failures stop before expensive native suites, while successful gates retain every existing test once. Gate already runs typecheck before test, so build output must never substitute for type checking.

Runner owns the exact three entrypoints, not a glob: examples/counting/browser/client.test.ts, voice-controller.test.ts and app.test.ts. Use one esbuild build with all three, platform=node, format=esm, .mjs outputs, metafile=true, and explicit client/bytes/contract aliases to THIS checkout. Use a fresh OS temporary output directory. Spawn `process.execPath --test` with the three produced .mjs files exactly once; inherit output, propagate nonzero exit/signal and clean temporary files after the child finishes. No TS stripping/registerHooks/version-specific source loader, external worktree alias, private credentials, dynamic fetch, network or tool-install fallback.

The current lock contains esbuild0.28.1, supporting Node>=18; Artroom requires Node>=22. The existing cached runner already used this locked bundler, but root package.json does not directly declare it. For permanent robustness, the future commission should explicitly declare the SAME locked version as a root devDependency and update only the lock's corresponding root dependency metadata through the normal reviewed dependency workflow. Do not fetch a different version or install merely to probe a tool. This makes the new root runner's dependency owned, rather than relying indefinitely on transitive hoisting. Planner must approve that manifest/lock metadata scope. If dependency declaration is declined, retain explicit initial checks; do not silently add a fragile undeclared runner.

A deliberate Vitest migration is an alternative, but larger: change all three test imports/semantics, add a separate counting-browser project and source aliases, then review test lifecycle equivalence. It is unnecessary for the initial robust node:test runner and must not modify/remove existing projects. No migration is commissioned here.

## Selection witness and failure behavior

Use the real build result, not a test that repeats a string list: validate metafile output entryPoint records form exactly the three distinct normalized source paths, one output each. Reject missing/duplicate/unexpected entries before launching Node. Check all three output files exist and bind their hashes to the source entrypoints; log one concise source-to-output manifest plus the actual Node argv. The resulting single Node run must contain the existing meaningful first-refusal gateway case and Explicit Arm controller case, plus the actual roster-removal app case, each once, with a successful terminal summary. This proves all three actual files were selected and executed; do not infer it from a total test count alone.

Review one bounded failure witness only after commission: omit one real compiled entrypoint from the runner selection and show the selection guard fails before Node; restore exact source. The ordinary real run itself supplies positive coverage; do not rerun all three files solely to check wiring. A real source test failure must propagate through npm test/npm run gate; the runner never masks it or marks an incomplete run passed. This is not a mutation sweep or a new per-field test matrix.

Type coverage is explicit: browser tsconfig includes main/app/view/speech/client/dispatcher/journal/storage/preview and all three test files, with DOM/Node types and composed package paths. A type failure stops the existing gate before tests. No giant new test census or active-source exception rewrite is needed for this follow-up.

